# "What were left" when "what" (the thing that) is the subject: it takes a
# singular verb. Our own rule, from a user report; examples are ours.
# why: Here "what" means "the thing that", which is singular: what was left, what was needed. A plural verb only follows in a direct question ("What were the results?").
expr done [left, given, needed, said, done, required, missing, wanted, offered, asked, expected, decided, agreed, planned, promised, ordered, bought, sent, owed, lost, saved, found, used]
expr main <(what were @done), were>

let message "Use `was` here: `what` (the thing that) takes a singular verb."
let description "Fixes `what were left` → `what was left`."
let kind "Agreement"
let becomes "was"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "I gave him what were left of the day before." "I gave him what was left of the day before."
test "We used what were needed for the repair." "We used what was needed for the repair."

allows "I gave him what was left of the day before."
allows "What were the results of the test?"
allows "Tell me what were your reasons."
